      "final_message": "I rewrote `parse_duration` in `solution.py` to follow the README, and both public tests pass (`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`: 2 tests, OK).\n\nThe old version didn't parse anything: it just returned the length of the string. The new one:\n- **Parses:** it matches the whole string against `(N h)?(N m)?(N s)?`, which enforces the hours-minutes-seconds order. It then adds up hours \u00d7 3600, minutes \u00d7 60 and seconds.\n- **Allowed digits:** only the plain digits 0\u20139 count. Leading zeros like `05m` work, but full-width digits such as `\uff11h` are rejected.\n- **Raises `ValueError`** for an empty string, the wrong order (`1m2h`), an unknown unit (`1d`), a unit with no number (`h`), a leading sign (`+1h`, `-1s`), or any extra characters, including a trailing space or newline.\n- **Raises `TypeError`** if the input isn't a string.\n\nI also ran a quick one-off check beyond the public tests. Valid inputs gave the right totals (`05m` \u2192 300, `2h` \u2192 7200, `10s` \u2192 10, `1h5s` \u2192 3605). Every invalid case listed above raised `ValueError`, and a non-string raised `TypeError`.\n\nDONE: Rewrote parse_duration as a whole-string regex match that returns total seconds and raises ValueError/TypeError as documented; public tests pass.",
