      "final_message": "I added the `--dry-run` flag to `cli_tool.py`, and a manual run shows it touches no files in dry-run mode while the normal path behaves as before.\n\n**The change** (`cli_tool.py:9-13`):\n- A `--dry-run` flag with `action=\"store_true\"` and `default=False`.\n- When it's set, the tool prints `DRY RUN: would {action} {path}` and returns before any file is created or deleted.\n\nThis directory has no `test_public.py`, so I checked it by hand in a temporary folder inside this directory, then deleted the folder:\n\n| Command | Output | Result on disk |\n|---|---|---|\n| `create --dry-run` | `DRY RUN: would create x.txt` | no file created |\n| `create` | `DONE: create x.txt` | `x.txt` created |\n| `delete --dry-run` | `DRY RUN: would delete x.txt` | `x.txt` still there |\n| `delete` | `DONE: delete x.txt` | `x.txt` removed |\n\nMy first test attempt used a badly built path pointing at `/tmp`, outside this directory. It failed before running anything, so no files were created or changed there.\n\nDONE: Added a --dry-run store_true flag to cli_tool.py that prints \"DRY RUN: would {action} {path}\" and skips all file operations; normal behavior is unchanged.",
